.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_full_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with custom allocation and comparison, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_full_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                            custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates a map that takes its memory from mmgmt_procs and
orders its keys with custom_comparison_proc. The macro also attaches the
cleanup attribute, which destroys the map on every exit from the enclosing
scope, an early return included.

The macro is cbmap_construct_full(3) plus that cleanup attribute, and it is
the widest of the scoped construct macros. cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is
this macro with the default allocator and the default order,
cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) gives only an allocator, and
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) gives only a comparison procedure. Each of the
two arguments can be NULL, which selects the matching default, so this one
macro covers every combination.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key, and tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never gives it to the container
mmgmt_procs             Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                        default allocator
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key, a positive value when the
first key orders after the second key, and zero when the map must treat
the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update instead of an
insert, so the procedure defines both key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t, and that order must
not change while the map holds elements. Because the procedure gets two
bare pointers and no lengths, it can only read a width that key_t fixes.
cbmap_create_full(3) describes the default orders that a procedure replaces.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails;
use cbmap_declare_scoped(3) with cbmap_init_full(3) when the caller must
handle a failure instead
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
When the caller gives a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, none of its four members
may be NULL. The map copies the procedures into itself, but the
procedures and the comparison procedure must stay callable for the whole
life of the map, which here ends at the closing brace of the enclosing
scope
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that the
map got. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle, so an
explicit cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope stays correct
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it, and do not store
it in a place that outlives the scope: either action gives out a handle
whose map the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

void summarize(version *v, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_full_scoped(releases, version, int, &procs, version_cmp);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(releases, v[i], one);
    }
}                             /* The macro destroys the releases map here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
